before:
Read and count this array.
```javascript
const tools = ["pencil", "ruler", "eraser"];
console.log(tools[0]);
console.log(tools.length);
console.log(tools[tools.length - 1]);
```
The output lines are `pencil`, `3`, and `eraser`. The last expression first calculates 3 minus 1, then reads index 2. Using length minus one lets you find the final entry even when the list changes size.

Here is a second complete example with an addition.
```javascript
const tools = ["pencil", "ruler", "eraser"];
tools.push("glue");
console.log(tools[tools.length - 1]);
console.log(tools.length);
console.log(tools[0]);
```
It displays `glue`, `4`, and `pencil`. The fourth entry occupies index 3. Adding at the end has not moved the first entry, and the original three entries are still present in their earlier order.

after:
